  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1210 AM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will persist into Friday, strengthening \nslightly over NM as the Baja low gets steered farther eastward into \nthe Mexican mainland via the subtropical jet. The 700 mb flow would \nrise to 15-30 kt while a weak lee-side surface low develops near the \nNM-CO border, and this would lead to breezy to windy conditions over \nthe central highlands and surrounding highland zones Friday \nafternoon. Downsloping winds would boost temperatures another 8 to \n14 degrees in the eastern plains while remaining zones also gain a \ncouple. \n\nThe northwest flow aloft is still modeled to increase more into \nSaturday with fair member-to-member consensus, however there are \nsubstantial differences regarding the sharpness of a northern to \ncentral Rockies shortwave trough. The 700 mb speeds look to rise to \n25 to 45 kt range, perhaps slightly stronger along the highlands, \nespecially near the central mountain chain. As for any surface low, \nthat would be strongly dependent on the strength and track of the \nshortwave trough with quite a bit of spread among deterministic and \neven ensemble means. Regardless, a notable increase in surface \nspeeds looks likely Saturday with gusts potentially closing in on a \n40-50 mph range over the highlands, particularly near the central \nmountain chain. Temperatures would gain a couple of degrees, pushing \ndaytime highs above normal in all areas and as many as 8 to 12 above \nclimatology in the far eastern plains. Precipitation would be \nincidental with the Saturday shortwave and most probable to be very \nlight and confined near and just east of the Raton Pass through \nSaturday night. \n\nWinds aloft look to turn more zonal into Sunday with the surface \nfront changing up the surface wind directions behind Saturday's \nshortwave passage. This dry frontal passage would be most notable in \nthe eastern plains where temperature readings would dip back just \nbelow normal going into the daytime Sunday. The front would likely \ndisrupt boundary layer mixing as well, allowing surface wind speeds \nto be much lighter on Sunday.\n\nThe next upstream disturbance will be another mainly dry and \nprogressive shortwave passage that will cause wind speeds aloft and \nat the surface to surge up on Monday. Minimal precipitation chances \nwould accompany and temperatures look to stay near to slightly above \nnormal both Monday and into Tuesday.",
